PG.notes("l225", "<p class=\"summary\">Defines an API as an agreed set of rules letting one program use another's data or functions, shows three usage patterns (GET data, POST data, trigger hardware), and names REST as the style to learn for the web. Ends with a Postman exercise that calls the Where the ISS at? API and plots the result in Google Maps.</p>\n<h3>What an API is</h3>\n<ul><li>API = Application Programming Interface: a set of rules and protocols defining how separate pieces of software talk to each other.</li><li>It acts as a translator or bridge: you do not need to know the other system's language, database or servers, only the documented requests it accepts and the responses it returns.</li><li>Key properties: communication, abstraction (hides internals), reusability, standardisation, and typical use of HTTP with JSON or XML formats.</li><li>Real examples: maps, social media data, payments.</li></ul>\n<h3>Three usage patterns</h3>\n<ul><li>Fetching data with GET: a diary site asks a weather provider for the weather at a latitude/longitude. The API docs say which inputs are accepted and how the reply is structured (for example a temperature in Celsius and a weather-type icon). Your site then shows the result.</li><li>Sending data with POST: a newsletter signup form sends the collected email to an email-marketing provider. The API defines the shape of the data to send and the responses for success or error.</li><li>Controlling hardware: an IoT watering device exposes a path such as /water; a request to that path triggers the valve, and the response might report the water released or soil moisture.</li><li>Common thread: both sides follow an agreed protocol so each knows what to send and expect.</li></ul>\n<h3>API styles and REST</h3>\n<ul><li>There are several architectural styles: REST, GraphQL, SOAP, gRPC and others. Each is a different rule set for designing an API.</li><li>Take a just-in-time approach: learn what you need when you need it. For web development that is REST.</li><li>A RESTful API follows a set of rules, the central one being that you use HTTP and its methods: GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE (covered earlier in the course).</li></ul>\n<h3>Reading API documentation</h3>\n<ul><li>Docs work like a menu: each endpoint lists its URL, the HTTP method, required inputs, and an example of the response structure.</li><li>Pick the endpoint that returns what you need, check required parameters (such as an ID), and copy the example request URL.</li><li>Postman is used to test requests with no front end or back end needed, so you can inspect the response before writing code.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Request used in the exercise (endpoint shape from the docs; ISS has satellite id 25544)</p><pre data-lang=\"txt\">GET https://api.wheretheiss.at/v1/satellites/25544</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Part of the JSON response that matters (illustrative values)</p><pre data-lang=\"json\">{\n  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;iss&quot;,\n  &quot;id&quot;: 25544,\n  &quot;latitude&quot;: 12.34,\n  &quot;longitude&quot;: -150.67\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Find the live location of the ISS</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Postman installed, the Where the ISS at? API documentation page open.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Make a GET request to the right endpoint and use the returned coordinates to locate the ISS in Google Maps.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Read the docs and ignore the endpoint that only lists tracked satellites.</li><li>Choose the endpoint that returns position and velocity for one satellite; the ISS id is 25544.</li><li>In Postman create a GET request to the example URL ending in /satellites/25544 and press Send.</li><li>Read latitude and longitude from the JSON response.</li><li>Paste 'latitude,longitude' (single comma, keep minus signs) into Google Maps search.</li><li>If Maps rejects it, check Google's guidance on coordinate formats.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A map pin over the ISS's current position (the lecture's example was the North Pacific).</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using the wrong endpoint (the satellite list gives no coordinates).</li><li>Dropping the minus sign from a coordinate, which moves the pin to another hemisphere.</li><li>Adding a space or other separator instead of a plain comma between the numbers.</li><li>Assuming every API is REST.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l226", "<p class=\"summary\">Covers the three parts of an API request URL: the endpoint, query parameters and path parameters, and distinguishes private from public APIs. Three Postman exercises against the course's Bored API practise each: GET /random, filter by type and participants, and fetch one activity by key.</p>\n<h3>Private vs public APIs</h3>\n<ul><li>Your own front end calling your own server is effectively a private API: undocumented and meant only for your client.</li><li>A public API is one a provider deliberately exposes and documents so other developers' servers can request data or actions.</li><li>This module is about consuming public APIs from your own server code.</li><li>The practice API is the course's Bored API (bored-api.appbrewery.com), holding activities with fields such as type, participants, price and a unique key.</li></ul>\n<h3>Endpoints</h3>\n<ul><li>URL structure: base URL, then a forward slash, then an endpoint. Each endpoint is a fixed route for a purpose, for example /random for a random activity and /filter for filtered lists.</li><li>Docs normally give an example request and sample response for each endpoint. Always read them first.</li><li>Public APIs often rate-limit to prevent abuse; this one allows about 100 requests per 15 minutes. If you get a too-many-requests response, wait.</li><li>Exercise 1: send GET base-URL/random in Postman; each send returns a different activity.</li></ul>\n<h3>Query parameters</h3>\n<ul><li>Appended after the endpoint to add optional key/value information, mostly for filtering, sorting and searching.</li><li>Syntax: start with ?, write key=value, and join further pairs with &amp;, for example ?a=1&amp;b=2&amp;c=3.</li><li>Parameter names are exact and come from the docs (the filter endpoint takes participants in the plural).</li><li>Postman's Params table builds the URL from key/value rows. In server code you must build the query string yourself, so practise typing it.</li><li>Exercise 2: GET /filter?type=social&amp;participants=2 returns social activities for two people. The docs list the seven allowed type values.</li></ul>\n<h3>Path parameters</h3>\n<ul><li>A variable segment placed after a slash in the path, used to identify one specific resource such as an ID or username.</li><li>Contrast: query parameters filter or search across many results; path parameters pinpoint a single item.</li><li>Every activity has a unique key. Request GET /activity/&lt;key&gt; to fetch that one, e.g. the movie marathon activity with key 5914292.</li><li>Handy when your site must always show a specific item.</li></ul>\n<h3>Next up</h3>\n<ul><li>The following lesson covers JSON, the usual format of API responses.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">The three request shapes from the lecture</p><pre data-lang=\"txt\">GET https://bored-api.appbrewery.com/random\nGET https://bored-api.appbrewery.com/filter?type=social&amp;participants=2\nGET https://bored-api.appbrewery.com/activity/5914292</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Same query built in JavaScript</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const params = new URLSearchParams({ type: &quot;social&quot;, participants: 2 });\nconst res = await fetch(`https://bored-api.appbrewery.com/filter?${params}`);\nconst activities = await res.json();</pre>\n<h2>The task: Three Bored API requests in Postman</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Postman open and the Bored API documentation page.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Practise endpoint, query parameter and path parameter requests.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create a GET request to the base URL plus /random and send it repeatedly to see different activities.</li><li>Create a GET to /filter, add type=social and participants=2 (first with the Params table, then by typing ?type=social&amp;participants=2 yourself).</li><li>Copy an activity's key from a response and GET /activity/&lt;key&gt; to retrieve exactly that activity.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A random activity per request; a filtered list of social two-person activities; and one specific activity matching the key.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using a second ? instead of &amp; for later query parameters.</li><li>Misspelling parameter names such as participant vs participants.</li><li>Exceeding the rate limit while testing.</li><li>Confusing path and query parameters: use query for filtering, path for identifying one resource.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l227", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) as the string format APIs use to send data, how it differs from a JS object, and how JSON.stringify and JSON.parse convert between the two. Ends with the Taco Town exercise: parse a recipe JSON string in an Express POST route and render the chosen recipe in EJS.</p>\n<h3>What JSON is</h3>\n<ul><li>JSON = JavaScript Object Notation: a text format for sending data over the internet that is both human-readable and compact. It is the usual format of API responses.</li><li>Its structure mirrors a JS object: key/value pairs separated by colons, comma-separated properties, arrays, and objects nested inside objects.</li><li>It is good at representing complex nested data (e.g. a person with an array of degrees, each degree an object).</li></ul>\n<h3>JSON vs a JavaScript object</h3>\n<ul><li>Layout is almost identical, but in JSON every key must be a double-quoted string. JS object keys do not need quotes.</li><li>Values can be strings, numbers, arrays, objects (and booleans/null).</li><li>The real difference: JSON is serialized, meaning it is one single string, usually because it is travelling across a network. A JS object is a live in-memory structure that the interpreter understands and that has relaxed syntax.</li><li>Analogy: a JS object is the assembled wardrobe, JSON is the flat-pack box. Same content, minimal size for transport, re-assembled at the destination.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Real JSON also forbids comments, trailing commas, single quotes and functions/undefined values; a JS object may contain all of those.</li></ul>\n<h3>Reading JSON: use a viewer</h3>\n<ul><li>Raw API JSON is usually minified onto one line, which is hard for humans to read.</li><li>Paste it into a JSON visualiser (recommended: jsonviewer.stack.hu, free) and open the Viewer tab to expand/collapse nested nodes.</li><li>In the taco example the viewer shows an array of three recipe objects; each has ingredients -&gt; protein, salsa and a toppings array of objects.</li><li>Viewer icons distinguish objects from arrays, which matters when deciding whether to drill with dot notation or loop.</li></ul>\n<h3>JSON.stringify and JSON.parse</h3>\n<ul><li>JSON.stringify(obj) serializes: turns a JS object into a JSON string, keeping all the quotes, colons and commas that encode the nesting. Use it before sending data out.</li><li>JSON.parse(jsonString) is the reverse: turns the JSON string back into a normal JS object (or array).</li><li>After parsing you use ordinary property access, e.g. data.ingredients, indexing like data[0], loops, and so on.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> JSON.parse throws a SyntaxError on invalid JSON, so wrap it in try/catch when the source is untrusted.</li></ul>\n<h3>Taco Town exercise: how the pieces connect</h3>\n<ul><li>index.js holds recipeJSON as one long string constant (standing in for a response from a third-party API, to avoid real requests for now). recipe.json has the same data pretty-printed for reference.</li><li>The page has a form posting to /recipe with three buttons that share name=&quot;choice&quot; and have values chicken, beef, fish.</li><li>body-parser (urlencoded) makes the choice available as req.body.choice.</li><li>A switch on req.body.choice selects index 0, 1 or 2 of the parsed array.</li><li>The solution stores the pick in a variable, then redirects to GET /, which renders solution.ejs passing it as recipe.</li><li>In EJS: if recipe exists show recipe.name and the ingredients, otherwise show a prompt h2. Drill down with recipe.ingredients.protein.name, .preparation, recipe.ingredients.salsa.name.</li><li>toppings is an array of objects, so loop over it (forEach) and print quantity and name per li.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Serialize and parse</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const wardrobe = { doors: 2, drawers: 3, color: &quot;oak&quot; };\nconst flat = JSON.stringify(wardrobe); // '{&quot;doors&quot;:2,&quot;drawers&quot;:3,&quot;color&quot;:&quot;oak&quot;}'\nconst back = JSON.parse(flat);         // object again\nconsole.log(back.doors);               // 2</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Shape of one recipe in the JSON (trimmed)</p><pre data-lang=\"json\">{\n  &quot;id&quot;: &quot;0001&quot;, &quot;type&quot;: &quot;taco&quot;, &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Chicken Taco&quot;, &quot;price&quot;: 2.99,\n  &quot;ingredients&quot;: {\n    &quot;protein&quot;: { &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Chicken&quot;, &quot;preparation&quot;: &quot;Grilled&quot; },\n    &quot;salsa&quot;: { &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Tomato Salsa&quot;, &quot;spiciness&quot;: &quot;Medium&quot; },\n    &quot;toppings&quot;: [\n      { &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Lettuce&quot;, &quot;quantity&quot;: &quot;1 cup&quot;, &quot;ingredients&quot;: [&quot;Iceberg Lettuce&quot;] }\n    ]\n  }\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Taco Town</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Starter folder with index.js (recipeJSON string, express, body-parser, empty POST /recipe), views/index.ejs, public/styles/main.css, recipe.json, solution.js and solution.ejs. Run npm install first.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Make the site behave like the solution: clicking a chicken/beef/fish button shows that taco's name and ingredient list; before any click it shows a prompt.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>cd into the project folder and run npm install, then run nodemon solution.js and open localhost:3000 to see the target behaviour. Do not read solution.js or solution.ejs.</li><li>Study the JSON structure (recipe.json or a JSON viewer): array of 3 recipes, each with ingredients.protein, ingredients.salsa, ingredients.toppings[].</li><li>In the app.post(&quot;/recipe&quot;) handler, read req.body.choice.</li><li>Parse recipeJSON with JSON.parse and choose the matching element (switch or lookup) for chicken, beef, fish.</li><li>Send the chosen recipe to the template (res.render with data, or store it and redirect to /).</li><li>In views/index.ejs, show an h2 prompt if no data exists; otherwise show the recipe name.</li><li>List protein name and preparation, the salsa name, and loop over toppings to output quantity + name as list items.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Initial page shows a 'pick your favourite taco' prompt; clicking an emoji button shows the recipe title and a ul such as 'Chicken, Grilled', 'Tomato Salsa', '1 cup of Lettuce', and so on.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">let data;\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.render(&quot;solution.ejs&quot;, { recipe: data });\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/recipe&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  switch (req.body.choice) {\n    case &quot;chicken&quot;: data = JSON.parse(recipeJSON)[0]; break;\n    case &quot;beef&quot;:    data = JSON.parse(recipeJSON)[1]; break;\n    case &quot;fish&quot;:    data = JSON.parse(recipeJSON)[2]; break;\n    default: break;\n  }\n  res.redirect(&quot;/&quot;);\n});\n\n/* solution.ejs essentials */\n// &lt;% if (locals.recipe) { %&gt;\n//   &lt;h2&gt;&lt;%= recipe.name %&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;\n//   &lt;ul&gt;\n//     &lt;li&gt;&lt;%= recipe.ingredients.protein.name %&gt;, &lt;%= recipe.ingredients.protein.preparation %&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n//     &lt;li&gt;&lt;%= recipe.ingredients.salsa.name %&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n//     &lt;% recipe.ingredients.toppings.forEach(t =&gt; { %&gt;\n//       &lt;li&gt;&lt;%= t.quantity %&gt; of &lt;%= t.name %&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n//     &lt;% }) %&gt;\n//   &lt;/ul&gt;\n// &lt;% } else { %&gt; &lt;h2&gt;Pick your favourite taco ingredient&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;% } %&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting to JSON.parse the string: indexing the string with [0] gives one character, not a recipe.</li><li>Using locals.recipe in EJS guards against a ReferenceError when the variable was never passed (first visit).</li><li>toppings is an array: use a loop, not dot notation. Protein and salsa are plain objects: use dot notation.</li><li>The reference solution keeps the choice in a module-level variable, so it is shared by all visitors; fine for a demo, wrong for real apps.</li><li>The reference repo's own index.ejs variant renders directly from the POST route (no redirect), so a refresh re-submits the form; the redirect pattern avoids that.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Do not confuse JS object literal syntax with JSON: single quotes or unquoted keys make JSON.parse fail.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l228", "<p class=\"summary\">Shows how an Express server can call a third-party API itself, first with Node's built-in https module (verbose) and then with the Axios package (one line). The exercise builds an 'I'm Bored' site that fetches a random activity, filters by type and participants via query parameters, and handles 404 errors.</p>\n<h3>Why the server makes requests</h3>\n<ul><li>Besides browsers and Postman, a Node/Express backend often needs to call another company's public API and use the response, e.g. to render a page.</li><li>Flow: your server sends a request to the provider's server, which replies with the data; your server then passes it to the template.</li></ul>\n<h3>The native way: https module</h3>\n<ul><li>https ships with Node, no install needed.</li><li>Configure an options object: the host/base URL, the path (endpoint) and the HTTP method.</li><li>Call https.request(options, callback). The response arrives as a stream of chunks, so you append each chunk to a string on the 'data' event.</li><li>On the 'end' event all chunks are in; run JSON.parse on the accumulated string to get an object, then pass it to the view.</li><li>Wrap parsing in try/catch, add an 'error' listener on the request for network failures, and call req.end() to actually send it.</li><li>Conclusion: it works but takes many lines of boilerplate.</li></ul>\n<h3>Axios</h3>\n<ul><li>Axios is an npm package that simplifies server-to-server (and browser) HTTP calls, much as Express simplifies Node's raw server code. It has tens of millions of weekly downloads, used as a popularity signal when choosing packages.</li><li>axios.get(url) takes the full URL (same as you would type in a browser) and returns a promise. Use .then/.catch or async/await.</li><li>response.data holds the body; when the API returns JSON, Axios has already parsed it into a JS object, so no JSON.parse is needed.</li><li>Errors (network failure or non-2xx status) reject the promise, so one try/catch handles them all in one place.</li><li>It has aliases for the common verbs: axios.get, .post, .put, .patch, .delete.</li><li>Install with npm i axios and import it: import axios from &quot;axios&quot;.</li></ul>\n<h3>I'm Bored exercise: how it works</h3>\n<ul><li>Dependencies: axios, body-parser, ejs, express. API: bored-api.appbrewery.com with /random (one activity) and /filter (array of activities matching optional type and participants queries).</li><li>GET /: await axios.get on /random, then render the page with data = response.data. Fields shown: activity, type, participants.</li><li>POST /: the form has two selects named type and participants, so req.body.type and req.body.participants arrive as strings; empty string means 'any' (the API treats empty queries as no filter).</li><li>Build the filter URL with a template literal: /filter?type=${type}&amp;participants=${participants}.</li><li>/filter returns an array, but the page shows one card, so pick a random element: result[Math.floor(Math.random() * result.length)].</li><li>No match makes the API answer 404, Axios rejects, and the catch block renders the page with a friendly error string.</li><li>In EJS use locals.data and locals.error to test whether each was passed; show the card when data exists, the error div when error exists.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Axios GET in an Express route (from the reference solution)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">app.get(&quot;/&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  try {\n    const response = await axios.get(&quot;https://bored-api.appbrewery.com/random&quot;);\n    res.render(&quot;solution.ejs&quot;, { data: response.data });\n  } catch (error) {\n    console.error(&quot;Failed to make request:&quot;, error.message);\n    res.render(&quot;solution.ejs&quot;, { error: error.message });\n  }\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Native https version (illustrative, own code)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import https from &quot;https&quot;;\nconst options = { hostname: &quot;bored-api.appbrewery.com&quot;, path: &quot;/random&quot;, method: &quot;GET&quot; };\nconst req = https.request(options, (resp) =&gt; {\n  let body = &quot;&quot;;\n  resp.on(&quot;data&quot;, (chunk) =&gt; (body += chunk));\n  resp.on(&quot;end&quot;, () =&gt; {\n    try { console.log(JSON.parse(body)); } catch (e) { console.error(e.message); }\n  });\n});\nreq.on(&quot;error&quot;, (e) =&gt; console.error(e.message));\nreq.end();</pre>\n<h2>The task: I'm Bored activity finder</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Folder with index.js, views/index.ejs, public styles, solution.js and solution.ejs; dependencies axios, body-parser, ejs, express. Run npm install, then nodemon solution.js to see the finished site.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Home page shows a random activity card; the form filters by type and participants and shows a random match; unmatched filters show 'No activities that match your criteria.'</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>npm install, run nodemon solution.js and try the finished site (random card, education + 1 person works, education + 2 people errors).</li><li>GET /: use axios.get on the /random endpoint inside an async handler; log response.data to learn its shape.</li><li>In index.ejs output the activity, type and participants from the passed data object, and add a block for an error message.</li><li>POST /: console.log(req.body) to see type and participants.</li><li>Request /filter with both values as query parameters via axios.get and a template literal.</li><li>Pick one random item from the returned array and render index.ejs with it.</li><li>In catch, log error.message and render with a user-facing 'No activities that match your criteria.' message (optionally branch on a 404 status for more specific messages).</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A card with title, type and participants appears on load; Go with a valid combination shows a random matching card; an impossible combination shows the error text and the server logs a 404.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">app.post(&quot;/&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  try {\n    const type = req.body.type;\n    const participants = req.body.participants;\n    const response = await axios.get(\n      `https://bored-api.appbrewery.com/filter?type=${type}&amp;participants=${participants}`\n    );\n    const result = response.data;\n    res.render(&quot;solution.ejs&quot;, {\n      data: result[Math.floor(Math.random() * result.length)],\n    });\n  } catch (error) {\n    console.error(&quot;Failed to make request:&quot;, error.message);\n    res.render(&quot;solution.ejs&quot;, { error: &quot;No activities that match your criteria.&quot; });\n  }\n});\n\n/* solution.ejs */\n// &lt;% if (locals.data) { %&gt;\n//   &lt;h2&gt;&lt;%= data.activity %&gt;&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;span&gt;&lt;%= data.type %&gt;&lt;/span&gt;\n//   &lt;span&gt;participants: &lt;%= data.participants %&gt;&lt;/span&gt;\n// &lt;% } %&gt;\n// &lt;% if (locals.error) { %&gt; &lt;div class=&quot;tag-error&quot;&gt;&lt;%= error %&gt;&lt;/div&gt; &lt;% } %&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting async/await: response would be a pending Promise and response.data undefined.</li><li>Using only .data of the response: the top-level Axios response object also has status, headers, etc.</li><li>/filter returns an array, so rendering it directly will not fit a single-card template; choose one element.</li><li>Any catch is treated as 'no matches' in the reference; network outages would show the same message. Check error.response?.status === 404 to distinguish (Extra).</li><li>In the reference repo the starter index.js already contains the finished logic and its catch renders solution.ejs; it diverges from a true blank starter. The author field in package.json is the repo owner's, ignore it.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Build query strings with axios params ({ params: { type, participants } }) to get automatic URL encoding.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l229", "<p class=\"summary\">Covers four levels of API access control (none, Basic, API key, Bearer token), how each is sent in a request, and the authentication vs authorization distinction. Learners first try each type in Postman against the Secrets API, then implement all four server-side with Axios.</p>\n<h3>Why authenticate</h3>\n<ul><li>If data is valuable, an API should check who is asking before releasing it. Failing that check gives a 401 Unauthorized response.</li><li>Four levels in increasing security: no auth, Basic auth, API key, token-based (OAuth style).</li><li>Authentication = proving who you are (login/registration). Authorization = being permitted to use the service, e.g. by holding an API key that may or may not be tied to a registered user.</li></ul>\n<h3>Level 0: no authentication</h3>\n<ul><li>Easy to adopt, inclusive; typical for public read-only data (the Bored API).</li><li>Abuse is limited with rate limiting, counting requests per IP per time window (the Bored API allows 100 per 15 minutes). That caps traffic but does not identify users.</li></ul>\n<h3>Level 1: Basic authentication</h3>\n<ul><li>Client supplies a username and password it registered with the provider.</li><li>Transport: the pair is joined as username:password, Base64-encoded, and sent in the Authorization header as the word Basic, a space, then the encoded string.</li><li>Base64 re-encodes the text's bytes using a 64-character alphabet; the output is slightly longer than the input (e.g. 3 chars become 4). It is an encoding, not encryption.</li><li>Demonstration: copy the header Postman generates and decode it on a Base64 decoder site to recover the plain username:password. That shows the weakness; it is acceptable only because providers serve over HTTPS, which encrypts traffic in transit.</li><li>Postman builds this header for you when Authorization type is Basic Auth (check the Headers tab). Axios does the same via its auth option.</li></ul>\n<h3>Level 2: API key</h3>\n<ul><li>Provider issues a key (in the Secrets API, a GET to the generate-api-key endpoint). The key goes with every request.</li><li>Placement depends on the API: sometimes a header, sometimes a query parameter. Read the docs. Here it is a query parameter named apiKey (capital K).</li><li>Benefits: provider can track and bill usage per key (e.g. Google Maps APIs bill by request count), cap usage, revoke and regenerate keys. A leaked key does not expose the user's password or payment details.</li><li>Secrets API /filter takes score (minimum embarrassment score, 1-10) plus apiKey.</li></ul>\n<h3>Level 3: token-based (Bearer) auth and OAuth</h3>\n<ul><li>User logs in once with username and password, the server issues a token, and the token alone is used for later calls. The API (and any third-party app) never handles the password afterwards.</li><li>OAuth 2.0 is the industry standard: a third-party app (e.g. weather app reading a user's calendar) sends the user to 'sign in with Google'; Google issues a token the app uses to act for that user within granted permissions.</li><li>In the Secrets API: POST username and password (form-urlencoded) to get-auth-token. Calling again returns the already-issued token.</li><li>Send it as header Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt; (capital B, one space). Used for GET /secrets/{id}.</li><li>Postman: Authorization type Bearer Token generates that header.</li></ul>\n<h3>Pagination (side note)</h3>\n<ul><li>GET /all returns secrets 10 per page and accepts a page query parameter, so huge result sets are sent in slices.</li></ul>\n<h3>Postman walk-through (Secrets API at secrets-api.appbrewery.com)</h3>\n<ul><li>Register: POST /register, body type x-www-form-urlencoded with keys username and password. Status 200 means success; keep the credentials.</li><li>Basic: GET /all (try page=2), Authorization tab type Basic Auth with those credentials.</li><li>API key: GET generate-api-key, then GET /filter?score=7 with apiKey as a query param (Postman Authorization tab 'API Key', add to Query Params).</li><li>Bearer: POST get-auth-token with credentials, then GET /secrets/2 with Bearer Token auth.</li></ul>\n<h3>Code with Axios</h3>\n<ul><li>No auth: await axios.get(API_URL + &quot;/random&quot;), then pass JSON.stringify(result.data) to the EJS view as content (just to display text).</li><li>Basic: second argument { auth: { username, password } }; Axios Base64-encodes and sets the header.</li><li>API key: second argument { params: { score: 5, apiKey } }, or write the query string into the URL. Both are valid.</li><li>Bearer: a config object { headers: { Authorization: <code>Bearer ${token}</code> } } as second argument.</li><li>Each route is wrapped in try/catch; on failure the reference sends status 404 with error.message.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">All four auth styles with Axios (credentials are placeholders)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const API_URL = &quot;https://secrets-api.appbrewery.com&quot;;\nconst yourUsername = &quot;&lt;username&gt;&quot;;\nconst yourPassword = &quot;&lt;password&gt;&quot;;\nconst yourAPIKey = &quot;&lt;api key&gt;&quot;;\nconst yourBearerToken = &quot;&lt;token&gt;&quot;;\n\napp.get(&quot;/noAuth&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  try {\n    const result = await axios.get(API_URL + &quot;/random&quot;);\n    res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, { content: JSON.stringify(result.data) });\n  } catch (error) { res.status(404).send(error.message); }\n});\n\napp.get(&quot;/basicAuth&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  try {\n    const result = await axios.get(API_URL + &quot;/all?page=2&quot;, {\n      auth: { username: yourUsername, password: yourPassword },\n    });\n    res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, { content: JSON.stringify(result.data) });\n  } catch (error) { res.status(404).send(error.message); }\n});\n\napp.get(&quot;/apiKey&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  try {\n    const result = await axios.get(API_URL + &quot;/filter&quot;, {\n      params: { score: 5, apiKey: yourAPIKey },\n    });\n    res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, { content: JSON.stringify(result.data) });\n  } catch (error) { res.status(404).send(error.message); }\n});\n\nconst config = { headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${yourBearerToken}` } };\n\napp.get(&quot;/bearerToken&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  try {\n    const result = await axios.get(API_URL + &quot;/secrets/2&quot;, config);\n    res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, { content: JSON.stringify(result.data) });\n  } catch (error) { res.status(404).send(error.message); }\n});</pre>\n<h2>The task: API Auth with Axios</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Folder with index.js (TODO 1-5), solution.js, views/index.ejs (four buttons and a response area); deps axios, express, ejs. First do the Postman steps to get your own username/password, API key and token.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Four buttons on localhost:3000 each call a different Secrets API endpoint using a different auth style and show the JSON in the page.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>npm i, then put your own username, password, API key and bearer token in the constants in solution.js and run nodemon solution.js to see the target behaviour.</li><li>TODO 1: fill in the four constants in index.js.</li><li>TODO 2 (/noAuth): axios.get the /random endpoint, render index.ejs with content = JSON.stringify(result.data).</li><li>TODO 3 (/basicAuth): GET /all for page 2 using the auth option.</li><li>TODO 4 (/apiKey): GET /filter for embarrassment score 5 or more, passing apiKey as a query parameter.</li><li>TODO 5 (/bearerToken): GET /secrets/{id} with an Authorization: Bearer header (the lecture's reference uses id 2 in the snippet; the starter text mentions 42, so check the endpoint output).</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Clicking each button prints a JSON string in the response box: a random secret, page 2 of all secrets, secrets with score &gt;= 5, and a single secret by id.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Base64 is reversible by anyone; Basic auth over plain HTTP exposes the password.</li><li>Bearer header format must be exactly 'Bearer ' plus the token; a missing space or lowercase scheme can fail.</li><li>API key placement varies: query param vs header. Wrong location gives 401/403.</li><li>Never commit real usernames, passwords, keys or tokens. The reference repo hard-codes the author's real values in index.js and solution.js; those are omitted here. (Extra) Load them from a .env file with dotenv instead.</li><li>Reference quirk: its index.js fetches /secrets/2 while the starter comment asks for another id; and error handling just returns the Axios message with a 404 status regardless of the real cause.</li><li>Reusing /get-auth-token returns the same token, so a 'new' token not appearing is normal.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l230", "<p class=\"summary\">Covers the full set of HTTP verbs (GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE) through Axios, comparing promise chaining with async/await. The exercise wires up a small Express + EJS app so five form buttons each call a different endpoint of the Secrets API using a bearer token.</p>\n<h3>Two ways to consume a promise</h3>\n<ul><li>Axios returns a promise. It can be handled with .then() / .catch() / .finally() chained on the call, or with async/await inside a try/catch.</li><li>With .then(), the callback runs once the promise resolves, and further .then() calls run in order. Long chains get hard to trace when debugging.</li><li>async/await (added to JavaScript in ES2017/ES8) marks a function async and puts await before the call, so execution pauses on that line until the promise settles and the code reads top to bottom like synchronous code.</li><li>Both styles are documented by Axios and are equivalent; the course standardises on async/await with try/catch.</li></ul>\n<h3>Axios method signatures</h3>\n<ul><li>axios.get(url[, config]) and axios.delete(url[, config]): two parameters, the URL and an optional config object.</li><li>axios.post(url[, data[, config]]), axios.put(url[, data[, config]]) and axios.patch(url[, data[, config]]): three parameters. The middle one is the request body (Axios docs call it data), like the URL-encoded fields a browser form would submit.</li><li>The config object is where authentication goes, e.g. headers with an Authorization value, plus other options from the Request Config docs: url, method, baseURL, params, auth (basic auth) and so on. A request can even be described entirely in the config instead of passing the URL first.</li><li>In the docs, square brackets mark the optional parameters of each alias.</li></ul>\n<h3>What each verb means in a REST API</h3>\n<ul><li>GET reads a resource.</li><li>POST creates a resource; the server usually assigns the id.</li><li>PUT replaces an existing resource, so the full set of fields must be sent; anything omitted is overwritten.</li><li>PATCH updates only the fields supplied and leaves the rest untouched.</li><li>DELETE removes the resource; no body is sent.</li><li>An API that supports all of these is called fully RESTful.</li></ul>\n<h3>The demo app and how it is wired</h3>\n<ul><li>The app is an Express server that renders index.ejs. A content variable is injected into the page, so every route re-renders the template with the stringified API response (or the error).</li><li>The form has three inputs: id, secret and score. Which inputs matter depends on the route (GET/DELETE need only the id; POST needs secret and score; PUT/PATCH need the id plus the fields to change).</li><li>Five submit buttons live in one form. Each button has its own formaction attribute, which overrides the form's action for that button, so the same form posts to /get-secret, /post-secret, /put-secret, /patch-secret or /delete-secret.</li><li>All five browser requests are HTML form POSTs to the Express server. The server then makes the real GET/POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE call to the remote API with Axios.</li><li>Input names match the field names the Secrets API expects (secret, score), so req.body can be forwarded as the request body unchanged. body-parser's urlencoded middleware populates req.body.</li><li>Default secrets run from id 1 to 50. Creating one yields id 51; the demo then reads it, PUTs a new score, PATCHes the score again, deletes it and confirms a GET now returns an error.</li></ul>\n<h3>Error handling pattern</h3>\n<ul><li>On success, result.data holds the API's JSON body; JSON.stringify turns it into text for the template.</li><li>On failure Axios throws; error.response.data holds the message the API sent back (for example not found for a missing id), which is rendered the same way so the user sees what went wrong.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> If the server is unreachable there is no error.response, so reading error.response.data would itself throw; a production version would guard for that.</li></ul>\n<h3>Read the docs first</h3>\n<ul><li>Before using any API, read the endpoints being used: required URL, params, body keys, auth, and the shape of the response. This prevents confusing errors later.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Shared setup in the exercise (token placeholder, never commit a real one)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\nimport axios from &quot;axios&quot;;\nimport bodyParser from &quot;body-parser&quot;;\n\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\nconst API_URL = &quot;https://secrets-api.appbrewery.com&quot;;\nconst yourBearerToken = &quot;&lt;PASTE_YOUR_TOKEN&gt;&quot;;\nconst config = {\n  headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${yourBearerToken}` },\n};\napp.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true }));</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">The already-working GET route used as the template</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">app.post(&quot;/get-secret&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const searchId = req.body.id;\n  try {\n    const result = await axios.get(API_URL + &quot;/secrets/&quot; + searchId, config);\n    res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, { content: JSON.stringify(result.data) });\n  } catch (error) {\n    res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, { content: JSON.stringify(error.response.data) });\n  }\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Button-level formaction in the form (illustrative)</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;form method=&quot;POST&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;input name=&quot;id&quot; /&gt;\n  &lt;input name=&quot;secret&quot; /&gt;\n  &lt;input name=&quot;score&quot; /&gt;\n  &lt;button formaction=&quot;/get-secret&quot;&gt;GET&lt;/button&gt;\n  &lt;button formaction=&quot;/post-secret&quot;&gt;POST&lt;/button&gt;\n  &lt;button formaction=&quot;/put-secret&quot;&gt;PUT&lt;/button&gt;\n  &lt;button formaction=&quot;/patch-secret&quot;&gt;PATCH&lt;/button&gt;\n  &lt;button formaction=&quot;/delete-secret&quot;&gt;DELETE&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;/form&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: 5.5 REST APIs: finish the four missing routes</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Download and extract the lesson, run npm i. index.js has the Express setup, config with a bearer token placeholder, the index.ejs view with the five-button form, and a working /get-secret route. The other four routes are empty.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Make POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE buttons each call the matching Secrets API endpoint and show the response (or error) in the content area, behaving like the instructor's solution.js demo.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Run npm i, then paste the bearer token obtained in the previous (authentication) lesson into the yourBearerToken constant.</li><li>Start the app with nodemon and open localhost:3000. Optionally run solution.js first to see the target behaviour.</li><li>Study /get-secret: try/catch, await axios.get(url, config), render result.data stringified, render error.response.data on failure.</li><li>/post-secret: call axios.post(API_URL + '/secrets', req.body, config). The body is the form data (secret and score); no id needed since the API assigns it.</li><li>/put-secret: read req.body.id, then axios.put(API_URL + '/secrets/' + id, req.body, config).</li><li>/patch-secret: same shape as PUT but with axios.patch.</li><li>/delete-secret: read req.body.id and call axios.delete(API_URL + '/secrets/' + id, config); only two arguments, no body.</li><li>Test the whole lifecycle: POST a new secret (id 51), GET 51, PUT with full data, PATCH one field, DELETE, then GET 51 again to see the not-found error. Also try PUT on an id that does not exist to check error display.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Each button displays the JSON returned by the API (created secret, updated secret, deletion confirmation). Invalid ids show the API's error text in the content box.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">app.post(&quot;/post-secret&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  try {\n    const result = await axios.post(API_URL + &quot;/secrets&quot;, req.body, config);\n    res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, { content: JSON.stringify(result.data) });\n  } catch (error) {\n    res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, { content: JSON.stringify(error.response.data) });\n  }\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/put-secret&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const searchId = req.body.id;\n  try {\n    const result = await axios.put(API_URL + &quot;/secrets/&quot; + searchId, req.body, config);\n    res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, { content: JSON.stringify(result.data) });\n  } catch (error) {\n    res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, { content: JSON.stringify(error.response.data) });\n  }\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/patch-secret&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const searchId = req.body.id;\n  try {\n    const result = await axios.patch(API_URL + &quot;/secrets/&quot; + searchId, req.body, config);\n    res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, { content: JSON.stringify(result.data) });\n  } catch (error) {\n    res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, { content: JSON.stringify(error.response.data) });\n  }\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/delete-secret&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const searchId = req.body.id;\n  try {\n    const result = await axios.delete(API_URL + &quot;/secrets/&quot; + searchId, config);\n    res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, { content: JSON.stringify(result.data) });\n  } catch (error) {\n    res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, { content: JSON.stringify(error.response.data) });\n  }\n});</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting to paste the bearer token (or leaving the placeholder) makes every route fail with an auth error.</li><li>Using the wrong arity: passing config as the second argument of axios.post/put/patch makes it be sent as the body; the body must come second and config third.</li><li>Forgetting the id in the URL for PUT/PATCH/DELETE.</li><li>PUT with missing fields wipes them; use PATCH for partial updates.</li><li>Accidentally committing a real bearer token (the reference repo shows one hard-coded; replace it with your own and keep it out of version control).</li><li>Not running npm i before starting the server.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l231", "<p class=\"summary\">End-of-module project: a small Express + EJS site (called Lisper) that calls the Secrets API /random endpoint with Axios and shows a random anonymous secret and its username on a styled card. No authentication is used, keeping the focus on consuming an API from a server.</p>\n<h3>Project overview</h3>\n<ul><li>A server-rendered site inspired by the anonymous-confession app Whisper. Each page load shows one random user's name; the secret text is revealed when hovering over the card (hover effect is already in the supplied CSS).</li><li>Only the /random endpoint of the Secrets API is used, so no bearer token, registration or login is needed.</li><li>Skills practised: Express routing and static files, EJS templating with variables, reading API docs, and using Axios with async/await.</li><li>Starting files: index.js with numbered hint comments, solution.js (finished version), views/index.ejs, public/styles/main.css and an image. The CSS can be replaced if the learner wants their own design.</li></ul>\n<h3>Template contract with EJS</h3>\n<ul><li>index.ejs expects exactly two variables: secret (printed inside the card paragraph with the escaping tag) and user (printed in a paragraph with class user).</li><li>The stylesheet is linked as styles/main.css, which only resolves because public is served as a static folder (the public part of the path is omitted in the URL).</li></ul>\n<h3>Server walkthrough (the six hints)</h3>\n<ul><li>1. Import express and axios (both are listed in package.json dependencies; run npm i first).</li><li>2. Create the app with express() and set port 3000.</li><li>3. app.use(express.static('public')) is built-in Express middleware that serves files from the named folder, so stylesheets and images can be requested relative to it.</li><li>4. Define app.get('/') as the home route.</li><li>5. Inside the handler, await axios.get on the /random URL. The URL can be written in full or split into a base URL and config; Axios methods return promises, so either chain .then() or make the handler async and use await.</li><li>The response body is at result.data and contains id, secret, emScore and username. Map the API keys to the template variable names: secret from result.data.secret, user from result.data.username. Logging result.data is a good way to confirm the keys.</li><li>Wrap the call in try/catch. In the catch block, log error.response.data for the developer (the message from the API) and respond with HTTP status 500 plus a generic message for visitors instead of exposing details.</li><li>6. app.listen(port, callback) with a console message such as server started on port 3000.</li><li>Run with nodemon index.js and visit localhost:3000; refreshing the page fetches a different random secret.</li></ul>\n<h3>API caveat</h3>\n<ul><li>The Secrets API is a teaching service, not production infrastructure. User data (registrations, tokens, secrets) is wiped regularly, roughly every day or two, so it should not be relied on for a real product.</li><li>The next section uses more robust public APIs suitable for portfolio projects.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Finished server (adapted from the reference solution; catch now sends a response)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\nimport axios from &quot;axios&quot;;\n\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\n\napp.use(express.static(&quot;public&quot;));\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  try {\n    const result = await axios.get(&quot;https://secrets-api.appbrewery.com/random&quot;);\n    res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, {\n      secret: result.data.secret,\n      user: result.data.username,\n    });\n  } catch (error) {\n    console.log(error.response.data);\n    res.status(500).send(&quot;Something went wrong on the server.&quot;);\n  }\n});\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; {\n  console.log(`Server is running on port ${port}`);\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Template section of views/index.ejs</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;h1&gt;What's your secret?&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;div class=&quot;card&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;&lt;%= secret %&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;p class=&quot;user&quot;&gt;&lt;%= user %&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Secrets Project (Lisper)</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Extracted project with index.js containing six hint comments, a finished views/index.ejs, public/styles/main.css and an image. solution.js runs the completed app.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Build an Express server that fetches a random secret from the Secrets API and renders its text and username into index.ejs.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>cd into the project folder and run npm i.</li><li>Optionally run nodemon solution.js and open localhost:3000 to see the target behaviour.</li><li>Read index.ejs to learn the two variables it needs: secret and user.</li><li>In index.js import express and axios, create the app, set port 3000.</li><li>Serve the public folder with express.static.</li><li>Add an async GET / route that awaits axios.get('https://secrets-api.appbrewery.com/random').</li><li>Render index.ejs passing secret: result.data.secret and user: result.data.username.</li><li>Add try/catch: log error.response.data and send a 500 with a friendly message.</li><li>Call app.listen with a log message, run nodemon index.js and refresh to see random secrets. Optionally extend the project.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A styled card showing a username; hovering reveals the secret. Each refresh shows a different random entry.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\nimport axios from &quot;axios&quot;;\n\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\n\napp.use(express.static(&quot;public&quot;));\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  try {\n    const result = await axios.get(&quot;https://secrets-api.appbrewery.com/random&quot;);\n    res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, {\n      secret: result.data.secret,\n      user: result.data.username,\n    });\n  } catch (error) {\n    console.log(error.response.data);\n    res.status(500).send(&quot;Something went wrong on the server.&quot;);\n  }\n});\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; {\n  console.log(`Server is running on port ${port}`);\n});</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Template variable names (secret, user) must match the keys passed to res.render; the API field is username, not user, so it needs mapping.</li><li>Forgetting express.static means the CSS and image return 404.</li><li>Forgetting await leaves result as a pending promise and result.data is undefined.</li><li>The reference repo's catch block only calls res.status(500) without sending anything, so the browser request hangs; add .send(...) (the lecture describes showing a message).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> error.response is undefined on network failures, so logging error.response.data can itself throw; log error.message as a fallback.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l232", "<p class=\"summary\">A motivation tip: announce your learning goals to other people so social accountability helps you stick with them.</p>\n<h3>Accountability tip</h3>\n<ul><li>Goals known only to you are easy to drop; telling others adds a social cost to quitting.</li><li>Make the intention concrete and public, for example a fixed daily time commitment over a set number of days, with regular progress check-ins.</li><li>Social platforms host an established community around this: the #100DaysOfCode and #codingnewbie hashtags, where learners log daily progress and encourage each other.</li><li>Benefits: a visible track record of what was done each day, plus encouragement from people going through the same thing.</li><li>Action: post your goal and begin logging progress using those hashtags.</li></ul>");
